      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_REM_0005_01",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR_ROTATIONAL_KINETIC_ENERGY",
          "title": "1. Rotational and Rolling Kinetic Energy",
          "contentMarkdown": "Rotational kinetic energy of a body of moment of inertia I rotating with angular velocity $$\\omega$$ is $$K_{rot} = \\frac{1}{2} I \\omega^2$$. For a body of mass M, radius R, and radius of gyration k rolling without slipping: $$K_{total} = K_{trans} + K_{rot} = \\frac{1}{2} M v_{cm}^2 \\left( 1 + \\frac{k^2}{R^2} \\right)$$.",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_REM_0005_69380DBB",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_REM_0005_B94D09A3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_REM_0005_02",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR_ROLLING_INCLINED_PLANE",
          "title": "2. Acceleration on Incline and Body Comparison",
          "contentMarkdown": "Linear acceleration of a body rolling down an incline of angle $$\\theta$$ without slipping is $$a_{cm} = \\frac{g \\sin \\theta}{1 + k^2/R^2}$$. The ratio $$k^2/R^2$$ is 0.4 for a solid sphere, 0.5 for a solid disc/cylinder, and 1.0 for a thin spherical shell or ring. Therefore, a solid sphere reaches the bottom with the greatest acceleration and in the shortest time.",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_REM_0005_B2EB39F9",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_REM_0005_2AA5F227",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_REM_0005_3FF28F20"
          ]
        }
      ],
